  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="commodity_acarigo_cbd87b129a5d4bce2a9c4dfbac662bc12868ddcb380855f25e842854d03c356bb6">
    <crm:P190_has_symbolic_content>agaric, name given to various corky species of Polyporus, a genus of fungi growing upon trees; of which P. officinalis, chiefly found on the Larch, the ‘Female Agarick’ of old writers, was renowned as a cathartic, and with P. fomentarius, and P. igniarius, ‘Male Agarick’ used as a styptic, as tinder, and in dyeing (Pegolotti 411, OED s.v. agaric n. and a.) [Jenks] | Mushroom-forming; parenthesomes imperforate or perforate (spore caps have openings); includes subclasses Agaricomycetidae and Phallomycetidae; contains 17 orders. [Source: Getty AAT]</crm:P190_has_symbolic_content>
    <crm:P2_has_type rdf:resource="http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object"/>
  </rdf:Description>
